V+=[
X(41,'DOE, VINE, PARROT, LAMP',LS('Lalita as a doe in a forest, a parrot in a cage, a lamp in a courtyard','DĪPIKĀ','lamp'),
 'The doe in the forest of hrīṃ; the vine at the basin of hrīṃ; the parrot in the cage of hrīṃ; the lamp in the courtyard of hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkārāraṇyahariṇī','<b>the doe in the forest of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārāvālavallarī','<b>the creeper round the basin of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārapañjaraśukī','<b>the parrot in the cage of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārāṅgaṇadīpikā','<b>the lamp in the courtyard of hrīṃ</b>')],
 'The parrot (śukī) is one of Lalitā’s favourite companions in her images, often perched on her hand or near her. A parrot in a cage that speaks only one word is an image of the devotee’s mind trained to repeat the sacred syllable.',
 'Āvāla is the basin of earth dug around the base of a tree to hold water; the creeper grows from it. The courtyard lamp, lit at dusk in Indian homes, is one of the most domestic images in the hymn: the Goddess as the light in the middle of ordinary life. Each name sets the syllable as a place and the Goddess as what lives there.',
 'Light a small lamp where you live. Ordinary places can be sacred too.',
 'The <b>lamp</b> in the courtyard.'),
X(42,'LIONESS, BEE, NECTAR, BLOSSOM',LS('Lalita as a lioness in a cave and a bee on a lotus','SIṂHĪ','sun'),
 'The lioness in the cave of hrīṃ; the bee on the lotus of hrīṃ; the nectar of the flower of hrīṃ; the blossom-cluster of the tree of hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkārakandarāsiṃhī','<b>the lioness in the cave of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārāmbhojabhṛṅgikā','<b>the bee on the lotus of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārasumanomādhvī','<b>the nectar in the flower of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārataramañjarī','<b>the flower-cluster on the tree of hrīṃ</b>')],
 'This verse ends the second group of hrīṃ names. Lioness, bee, honey and blossom: power, delight, sweetness and beauty, all found inside the seed-syllable.',
 'The lioness in a cave is fierce and protective; the bee on a lotus, gentle and absorbed. Together they say that the same practice can give courage and sweetness. Mañjarī is a cluster of blossoms at a branch tip, as on a mango tree in spring: the flowering of the tree that grows from the seed. The imagery prepares for the final group of hrīṃ names at the end of the hymn.',
 'The same practice can make you braver and gentler at once.',
 '<b>Lioness</b> and <b>bee</b>.'),
X(43,'THE GOAL OF ALL VEDĀNTA',LS('Lalita above open books of the Upanishads','VEDĀNTA','book'),
 'Named by the syllable sa; of equal essence; praised by all the Āgamas; the ground of the meaning of all Vedānta; support of the real and the unreal.',
 [('sakārākhyā','<b>named by the syllable sa</b>'),('samarasā','<b>of equal, united essence</b>'),('sakalāgamasaṃstutā','<b>praised by all the scriptures</b>'),('sarvavedāntatātparyabhūmiḥ','<b>the ground of the purport of all Vedānta</b>'),('sadasadāśrayā','<b>support of being and non-being</b>')],
 'The last sa-group begins. Its names are among the most philosophical in the Triśatī, describing the Goddess in the language of Vedānta.',
 'Tātparya, purport, is the technical term for what a text finally intends. To call her the ground (bhūmi) of the purport of all the Upaniṣads is to say that the Upaniṣads all point to her. Samarasa, “of equal taste”, describes the union of Śiva and Śakti in which neither is greater: a key word in Śrīvidyā. Sat and asat, being and non-being, both rest on her.',
 'Read deeply enough and many paths point to the same place.',
 'What the <b>Upaniṣads</b> point to.'),
X(44,'TRUTH, CONSCIOUSNESS, BLISS',LS('Lalita glowing, sages meditating, Shiva beside her','SACCIDĀNANDĀ','light'),
 'The whole; truth, consciousness and bliss; the goal to be attained; giver of the good destination; meditated on by Sanaka and the sages; wife of Sadāśiva.',
 [('sakalā','<b>the complete, with all parts</b>'),('saccidānandā','<b>being, consciousness, bliss</b>'),('sādhyā','<b>the goal to be reached</b>'),('sadgatidāyinī','<b>giver of the true destination</b>'),('sanakādimunidhyeyā','<b>meditated on by Sanaka and other sages</b>'),('sadāśivakuṭumbinī','<b>household wife of Sadāśiva</b>')],
 'Saccidānanda, being-consciousness-bliss, is the most famous Vedāntic description of Brahman. The Triśatī gives it directly to the Goddess.',
 'Sakalā is a careful word: Śiva without form is niṣkala, “without parts”; with form and power he is sakala. She is the sakala, the whole as it appears. Sanaka and his three brothers are eternal child-sages who meditate on the Absolute. Sadāśivakuṭumbinī, Sadāśiva’s housewife, sets this cosmic theology inside a homely image: the supreme pair as a household.',
 'The highest truth and the most homely love are not far apart.',
 '<b>Truth, consciousness, bliss</b>.'),
X(45,'THE GROUND OF ALL',LS('Lalita as the ground beneath a world, nothing above her','ADHIṢṬHĀNA','mountain'),
 'Whose form is the substratum of all; whose form is truth; of balanced form; maker of the whole universe; without equal or superior.',
 [('sakalādhiṣṭhānarūpā','<b>the substratum of everything</b>'),('satyarūpā','<b>whose form is truth</b>'),('samākṛtiḥ','<b>of even, balanced form</b>'),('sarvaprapañcanirmātrī','<b>maker of the whole universe</b>'),('samanādhikavarjitā','<b>without any equal or superior</b>')],
 'Adhiṣṭhāna is a Vedānta term for the ground on which an appearance rests, like the rope on which a snake is mistakenly seen. To call her the adhiṣṭhāna of all is to say that the world appears on her as its reality.',
 'Samanādhikavarjitā echoes the Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad (6.8), which says of the supreme there is none equal or greater (na tat samaś cābhyadhikaś ca dṛśyate). Samākṛti, of balanced form, is read as even-tempered, equal to all, or of perfectly proportioned beauty.',
 'Look for what everything else stands on. That is what matters most.',
 'The <b>ground</b> of all.'),
X(46,'BEYOND ATTACHMENT',LS('Lalita high above, unattached, Kameshvara gazing at her','KĀVYALOLĀ','book'),
 'Highest of all; free from attachment; with qualities; giver of all desires. Who makes the syllable ka; fond of poetry; who enchants the mind of Kāmeśvara.',
 [('sarvottuṅgā','<b>highest of all</b>'),('saṅgahīnā','<b>unattached</b>'),('saguṇā','<b>possessing qualities, with form</b>'),('sakaleṣṭadā','<b>giver of everything desired</b>'),('kakāriṇī','<b>maker of the syllable ka</b>'),('kāvyalolā','<b>delighting in poetry</b>'),('kāmeśvaramanoharā','<b>who captivates Kāmeśvara’s mind</b>')],
 'The sa-group ends and the last ka-group begins. This final ka-group is filled with Kāmeśvara, the form of Śiva as the divine lover and Lalitā’s consort.',
 'Saṅgahīnā and saguṇā sit side by side: unattached, yet with qualities. The Goddess is both the formless and the one with form. Kāvyalolā, fond of poetry, is a charming name: the hymn itself is poetry, so she delights in it. Śrīvidyā has always attracted poets, from the Saundaryalaharī to Muttusvāmi Dīkṣitar’s songs.',
 'Freedom and warmth can live together: unattached, yet fond of poetry.',
 'She loves <b>poetry</b>.'),
X(47,'KĀMEŚVARA’S LIFE-BREATH',LS('Lalita on Kameshvara’s lap, embraced','KĀMEŚVARA','heart'),
 'The life-breath of Kāmeśvara; who dwells on Kāmeśa’s lap; whose limbs are embraced by Kāmeśvara; who gives joy to Kāmeśvara.',
 [('kāmeśvaraprāṇanāḍī','<b>the life-current of Kāmeśvara</b>'),('kāmeśotsaṅgavāsinī','<b>who sits on Kāmeśa’s lap</b>'),('kāmeśvarāliṅgitāṅgī','<b>embraced by Kāmeśvara</b>'),('kāmeśvarasukhapradā','<b>who gives happiness to Kāmeśvara</b>')],
 'Verses 47 to 50 are a garland of names built on Kāmeśvara, eighteen in a row. In the Śrīcakra, the divine pair Kāmeśvara and Kāmeśvarī sit together at the central point, the bindu.',
 'The repeated name is not monotony but devotion: the poet turns the relationship this way and that. She is his prāṇa, his breath, without which he is lifeless, which echoes the Saundaryalaharī’s claim that Śiva cannot stir without Śakti. The union of the pair is the Śrīvidyā picture of reality: consciousness and its power, never apart.',
 'In a true partnership each is the other’s breath.',
 'Kāmeśvara’s <b>breath</b>.'),
X(48,'THE FRUIT OF HIS TAPAS',LS('Shiva in meditation, Lalita appearing before him as the fruit of his penance','TAPAḤSIDDHI','fire'),
 'Kāmeśvara’s beloved; his playful companion; the fulfilment of Kāmeśvara’s penance; dear to Kāmeśvara’s heart.',
 [('kāmeśvarapraṇayinī','<b>beloved of Kāmeśvara</b>'),('kāmeśvaravilāsinī','<b>who plays with Kāmeśvara</b>'),('kāmeśvaratapaḥsiddhiḥ','<b>the attainment of Kāmeśvara’s austerity</b>'),('kāmeśvaramanaḥpriyā','<b>dear to Kāmeśvara’s mind</b>')],
 'Tapaḥsiddhi, the fruit of austerity, inverts the usual Purāṇic story in which Pārvatī performs tapas to win Śiva. Here she is the fruit of his tapas.',
 'In Śrīvidyā this has a philosophical sense: Śiva as pure consciousness “meditates”, and what arises is his power, Śakti, who makes him the Lord. Vilāsa, play, recalls the name Lalitā itself: creation as divine play. Each of these names makes the relationship mutual: she is his beloved and also the reason he is Lord.',
 'Long effort is finally rewarded with something given, not taken.',
 'The <b>fruit</b> of his tapas.'),
X(49,'LADY OF HIS HOUSE',LS('Lalita as mistress of a radiant palace, Kameshvara beside her','GṚHEŚVARĪ','crown'),
 'Lady of Kāmeśvara’s life; who enchants Kāmeśvara; Kāmeśvara’s knowledge of Brahman; mistress of Kāmeśvara’s house.',
 [('kāmeśvaraprāṇanāthā','<b>queen of Kāmeśvara’s life</b>'),('kāmeśvaravimohinī','<b>who enchants Kāmeśvara</b>'),('kāmeśvarabrahmavidyā','<b>Kāmeśvara’s knowledge of Brahman</b>'),('kāmeśvaragṛheśvarī','<b>mistress of Kāmeśvara’s house</b>')],
 'Gṛheśvarī, lady of the house, is a homely name for the cosmic queen. Lalitā’s “house” in the Lalitopākhyāna is the Cintāmaṇi Gṛha, a palace of wish-jewels at the heart of the Śrīpura on Meru.',
 'Kāmeśvarabrahmavidyā is the deepest name of the four: she is the knowledge by which Kāmeśvara knows himself as Brahman. Śakti is not just Śiva’s companion but his self-awareness. The pairing of the intimate (house, life, enchantment) with the highest (Brahman-knowledge) is typical of the Triśatī.',
 'The deepest knowledge often lives in the most ordinary rooms.',
 'His <b>knowledge of himself</b>.'),
X(50,'KĀMEŚVARĪ OF KĀMAKOṬI',LS('Lalita in a temple, Kanchi Kamakshi style, a seated goddess','KĀMAKOṬI','crown'),
 'Who gives joy to Kāmeśvara; the great Goddess of Kāmeśvara; Kāmeśvarī; who dwells at Kāmakoṭi; giver of desired things.',
 [('kāmeśvarāhlādakarī','<b>who delights Kāmeśvara</b>'),('kāmeśvaramaheśvarī','<b>great Goddess of Kāmeśvara</b>'),('kāmeśvarī','<b>Kāmeśvarī herself</b>'),('kāmakoṭinilayā','<b>who dwells at Kāmakoṭi</b>'),('kāṅkṣitārthadā','<b>giver of longed-for things</b>')],
 'The ka-group ends with Kāmakoṭinilayā: “dwelling at Kāmakoṭi”. Kāmakoṭi is the name of the sanctum of Kāmākṣī at Kāñcīpuram, and of the Kāñcī Maṭha’s pīṭha. Kāmākṣī is identified with Lalitā.',
 'The name is also read inwardly: kāma-koṭi, the summit or end of desire, the place where all desire is fulfilled. The Kāñcī tradition holds that Ādi Śaṅkara installed a Śrīcakra before Kāmākṣī, and the Triśatī is recited there in worship.',
 'The end of desire is not emptiness. The hymn calls it a home.',
 'At <b>Kāmakoṭi</b>, Kāñcī.'),
X(51,'ALL ATTAINED',LS('Lalita, serene, having attained all things','LABDHA','gift'),
 'Maker of the syllable la; who has attained form; who has attained wisdom; who has attained her wishes; far from the mind that has gained sin; hard to reach for the mind that has gained ego.',
 [('lakāriṇī','<b>maker of the syllable la</b>'),('labdharūpā','<b>who has taken form</b>'),('labdhadhīḥ','<b>who has attained wisdom</b>'),('labdhavāñchitā','<b>whose wishes are fulfilled</b>'),('labdhapāpamanodūrā','<b>far from the sin-laden mind</b>'),('labdhāhaṅkāradurgamā','<b>hard to reach for the proud mind</b>')],
 'The final la-group is almost all labdha names: labdha means obtained, attained. Twenty names describe the Goddess as one who has every attainment.',
 'Two names stand out because they turn to us: she is far from the mind full of sin and hard to reach for the mind full of ego. The labdha that sin and pride “attain” is the wrong kind of gain, and it pushes her away. Commentators read the whole group as a contrast between what she has gained and what we cling to.',
 'Some gains push away what we really want. Pride is one.',
 'Hard to reach for the <b>proud</b>.'),
X(52,'POWER, BODY, YOUTH',LS('Lalita radiant and youthful, power flowing from her','YAUVANA','star'),
 'Who has attained power; who has taken a body; who has risen in sovereignty; who has grown; who has taken up play; who has youth.',
 [('labdhaśaktiḥ','<b>who has attained power</b>'),('labdhadehā','<b>who has taken a body</b>'),('labdhaiśvaryasamunnatiḥ','<b>risen high in sovereignty</b>'),('labdhavṛddhiḥ','<b>who has grown</b>'),('labdhalīlā','<b>who has taken up play</b>'),('labdhayauvanaśālinī','<b>radiant with youth</b>')],
 'Labdhadehā, she who has taken a body, recalls her birth in the Lalitopākhyāna: she rose from the fire of the gods’ sacrifice in full form, already grown, already armed.',
 'Līlā, divine play, is the reason for creation in many Hindu philosophies: the divine creates not from need but from fullness, as a happy child plays. Yauvana, youth, is the perpetual state of the Goddess: Tripurasundarī is pictured as sixteen, ever young, which is also the name of her mantra form Ṣoḍaśī.',
 'Play is not childish. It is what fullness does.',
 'Ever <b>young</b>, always at play.'),
X(53,'EVERY BEAUTY',LS('Lalita at her most beautiful, many scriptures around her','SAUNDARYA','gift'),
 'Who has attained supreme beauty in every limb; who has attained graceful charm; who has attained love; who has gained a husband; who is established in the many scriptures.',
 [('labdhātiśayasarvāṅgasaundaryā','<b>who has surpassing beauty in every limb</b>'),('labdhavibhramā','<b>who has graceful charm</b>'),('labdharāgā','<b>who has love</b>'),('labdhapatiḥ','<b>who has a husband</b>'),('labdhanānāgamasthitiḥ','<b>established in the many scriptures</b>')],
 'The Saundaryalaharī, “Waves of Beauty”, ascribed to Ādi Śaṅkara, is the great poem on the Goddess’s beauty. Its name echoes this verse: beauty (saundarya) in every limb.',
 'Labdhapati, she who has a husband, sounds simple, but in the theology it means that Śakti has attained Śiva, and through her Śiva becomes the Lord. Labdhanānāgamasthiti: she is found established in many scriptures, Vedic and Tantric. Her beauty is not decoration but an expression of fullness.',
 'Beauty that comes from inner fullness never fades.',
 'Beauty in <b>every limb</b>.'),
X(54,'THE DOVE ON THE PALACE',LS('A dove perched on the top of a palace, Lalita glowing','KAPOTIKĀ','crown'),
 'Who has attained enjoyment; who has attained happiness; filled with joy attained. Whose form is hrīṃ; the dove on the roof of the mansion of hrīṃ.',
 [('labdhabhogā','<b>who has attained enjoyment</b>'),('labdhasukhā','<b>who has attained happiness</b>'),('labdhaharṣābhipūritā','<b>filled with joy</b>'),('hrīṃkāramūrtiḥ','<b>whose embodiment is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārasaudhaśṛṅgakapotikā','<b>the dove on the rooftop of the mansion of hrīṃ</b>')],
 'The la-group closes and the last hrīṃ group, twenty names, begins. These final names return to the images of verses 39 to 42, now even richer.',
 'Saudha is a white palace or mansion; its highest pinnacle is the śṛṅga, and a dove (kapotikā) sits there. The dove at the top of the house is a homely, peaceful image: she is the gentlest thing at the highest point. Bhoga, sukha, harṣa, enjoyment, happiness and joy, end the labdha series on a note of delight.',
 'At the top of every achievement, look for peace. That is where she sits.',
 'The <b>dove</b> on the rooftop.'),
X(55,'NECTAR OF THE MILK-OCEAN',LS('Lalita rising from a milky ocean on a lotus, a parrot in a tree','SUDHĀ','ocean'),
 'The nectar of the milk-ocean of hrīṃ; Lakṣmī of the lotus of hrīṃ; the flame of the jewel-lamp of hrīṃ; the mynah in the tree of hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkāradugdhābdhisudhā','<b>the nectar of the milk-ocean of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārakamalendirā','<b>the Lakṣmī on the lotus of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāramaṇidīpārciḥ','<b>the flame of the jewel-lamp of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārataruśārikā','<b>the mynah in the tree of hrīṃ</b>')],
 'The milk ocean recalls the churning, from which both the nectar of immortality (sudhā) and Lakṣmī (Indirā) rose. The syllable is the ocean; she is what rises from it.',
 'A jewel-lamp (maṇidīpa) needs no oil: its flame is the jewel’s own light. She is that self-shining light inside the seed-syllable. The śārikā, a mynah bird, is another talking bird of the Goddess’s court; like the parrot of verse 41, it is the devotee’s mind trained to speak her name.',
 'What is churned with patience gives nectar.',
 'The <b>nectar</b> from the churning.'),
X(56,'THE DANCER',LS('Lalita dancing in a hall, reflected in a mirror','NARTAKĪ','light'),
 'The gem in the casket of hrīṃ; reflected in the mirror of hrīṃ; the sword in the sheath of hrīṃ; the dancer in the hall of hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkārapeṭakamaṇiḥ','<b>the jewel in the casket of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārādarśabimbitā','<b>reflected in the mirror of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārakośāsilatā','<b>the sword in the scabbard of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārāsthānanartakī','<b>the dancer in the court of hrīṃ</b>')],
 'Casket, mirror, scabbard and court hall: four containers, each holding something precious, bright, sharp or alive. The pattern continues: the syllable holds, she is held.',
 'The mirror image is philosophical: a reflection is the original seen in a medium. She is seen in the syllable as a face in a mirror, present but not limited by it. The sword in its sheath is power at rest, ready but not drawn. The dancer in the hall returns to the theme of divine play, līlā.',
 'Power can rest in its sheath. Not every strength must be drawn.',
 'The <b>sword</b> in its sheath.'),
X(57,'PEARL AND PUPPET',LS('A pearl in an oyster, a coral figure on a golden pillar','MUKTĀMAṆI','gift'),
 'The pearl in the oyster of hrīṃ; awakened by hrīṃ; the coral figurine on the golden pillar made of hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkāraśuktikāmuktāmaṇiḥ','<b>the pearl in the oyster of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārabodhitā','<b>awakened by hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāramayasauvarṇastambha…','<b>on the golden pillar made of hrīṃ</b>'),('…vidrumaputrikā','<b>the coral figurine</b>')],
 'Temple pillars in South India are often carved with figurines (sālabhañjikās) of graceful women. Here the pillar is gold and made of the syllable, and the figure on it is coral: the Goddess as a carved image on the pillar of the mantra.',
 'The pearl in the oyster grows slowly from a grain of sand: an old image for realisation formed slowly inside a practice. Hrīṃkārabodhitā, awakened by hrīṃ, is read both ways: she awakens through the syllable in the devotee, and the devotee is awakened by her through it.',
 'Pearls take time. So does anything worth having inside.',
 'The <b>pearl</b> in the shell.'),
X(58,'THE GIFT AT THE SACRIFICE',LS('A sacrificial fire, a wish-creeper in a heavenly garden','KALPAKAVALLARĪ','tree'),
 'The Upaniṣad of the Veda of hrīṃ; the dakṣiṇā of the sacrifice of hrīṃ; the new wish-creeper in the Nandana garden of hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkāravedopaniṣad','<b>the Upaniṣad of the Veda that is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārādhvaradakṣiṇā','<b>the sacrificial gift of the rite that is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāranandanārāma…','<b>in the Nandana garden of hrīṃ</b>'),('…navakalpakavallarī','<b>the fresh wish-granting creeper</b>')],
 'The Upaniṣad is the essence and end of a Veda; dakṣiṇā is the gift given at the end of a sacrifice, which makes it complete. The Goddess is both: the inner meaning of the mantra and the reward that completes its practice.',
 'Nandana is Indra’s pleasure garden in heaven, where the wish-granting trees grow. A fresh kalpaka creeper there is the newest, most abundant giver of wishes. The verse brings Vedic ritual, Upaniṣadic wisdom and heavenly abundance together in the syllable.',
 'A practice is complete only when it gives something back.',
 'The <b>Upaniṣad</b> of the seed.'),
X(59,'THE GAṄGĀ AND THE KAUSTUBHA',LS('The Ganga flowing from the Himalaya, a jewel rising from the ocean','PARASAUKHYA','ocean'),
 'The Gaṅgā of the Himalaya of hrīṃ; the Kaustubha jewel of the ocean of hrīṃ; the whole wealth of the mantra hrīṃ; giver of the supreme bliss of hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkārahimavadgaṅgā','<b>the Gaṅgā from the Himalaya of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārārṇavakaustubhā','<b>the Kaustubha jewel of the ocean of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāramantrasarvasvā','<b>the entire wealth of the hrīṃ mantra</b>'),('hrīṃkāraparasaukhyadā','<b>giver of the highest bliss of hrīṃ</b>')],
 'The 300th name ends the hymn with bliss. The Gaṅgā flows from the Himalaya, and Pārvatī, the Himalaya’s daughter, is Gaṅgā’s sister; the Kaustubha is the jewel that rose from the churned ocean and is worn by Viṣṇu. Śiva and Viṣṇu images meet in the last verse.',
 'After the hymn, the uttarapīṭhikā, the closing section, has Hayagrīva explain the worth of the names, link them to the Śrīcakra, and praise their recitation above other hymns. It also says the hymn should not be taught to the faithless. We describe this section but do not reproduce it. The last name, giver of supreme bliss, is the hymn’s promise in one word.',
 'Let the last word of anything you do be joy.',
 'Giver of <b>supreme bliss</b>.'),
]
